1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,279
JWT told you how a harbor resource server
trusts a bearer token. OAuth2 answers a
different question: how did the client get
permission to hold that token in the first
place?

2
00:00:11,459 --> 00:00:23,219
Think about a calendar app that wants to
read a planner’s harbor appointments —
vessel windows, berth holds — on the
user’s behalf. You do not want that
calendar app to collect the harbor
password.

3
00:00:23,339 --> 00:00:37,067
You want the user to log in at the harbor
identity provider, approve a scope like
harbor.calendar.read, and send the
calendar app an access token that
represents that limited grant. OAuth2 is
that delegation protocol.

4
00:00:37,187 --> 00:00:47,195
Roles have names: resource owner (the
user), client (the calendar app),
authorization server (issues tokens),
resource server (the harbor API that
accepts tokens).

5
00:00:47,315 --> 00:01:00,419
The authorization code flow is the one you
should narrate for browser and
confidential clients. The client redirects
the user to the authorization server with
client id, redirect URI, scopes, and a
state value.

6
00:01:00,539 --> 00:01:08,003
The user authenticates and consents. The
authorization server redirects back with a
short-lived code.

7
00:01:08,123 --> 00:01:17,099
The client exchanges that code — plus its
client secret, for confidential clients —
at the token endpoint for an access token
(and often a refresh token).

8
00:01:17,219 --> 00:01:32,268
The client then calls the harbor API with
Authorization: Bearer .... PKCE adds a
code challenge so public clients (mobile,
SPA) are safer even without a secret.
Spring Security supports both sides you
usually build.

9
00:01:32,388 --> 00:01:40,716
As a resource server, you validate access
tokens — the JWT path from the previous
episode, or opaque token introspection.

10
00:01:40,835 --> 00:01:48,323
As an OAuth2 client, Boot can drive the
redirect, code exchange, and authorized
WebClient / RestClient calls.

11
00:01:48,444 --> 00:02:01,427
As an authorization server, Spring
Authorization Server is a separate project
when you issue tokens yourself instead of
buying Okta, Auth0, Keycloak, or a cloud
IdP. Look at this java for a moment.

12
00:02:01,548 --> 00:02:07,236
When the user hits a protected calendar
page, oauth2Login sends them to the
provider.

13
00:02:07,355 --> 00:02:17,843
After callback success, Spring establishes
a local Authentication (often an
OAuth2AuthenticationToken) and can store
authorized client tokens for later harbor
API calls.

14
00:02:17,963 --> 00:02:31,692
Separately, the harbor API microservice
might only enable oauth2ResourceServer and
never redirect browsers at all — it only
trusts bearer tokens. Pick the hat your
process is wearing. Grant types matter.

15
00:02:31,812 --> 00:02:44,388
Authorization code (+ PKCE) for user-
delegated access. Client credentials when
a service talks to another service with no
user present — the token represents the
client, not Alice.

16
00:02:44,508 --> 00:02:59,484
Implicit and password grants are legacy
for most new work. A frequent
misunderstanding is treating “we added
oauth2Login” as the same as securing a
JSON API for truckers. Login redirect
flows are for interactive clients.

17
00:02:59,604 --> 00:03:04,692
Machine APIs usually want resource-server
validation of access tokens.

18
00:03:04,812 --> 00:03:17,004
Another misunderstanding is confusing the
access token with proof of who the user is
for your own profile screen — access
tokens authorize API calls; identity
claims for login UX are the OpenID Connect
layer next.

19
00:03:17,124 --> 00:03:26,652
Today we placed JWT inside a protocol:
clients obtain tokens through redirects
and token endpoints; resource servers
consume them; scopes limit the grant.

20
00:03:26,772 --> 00:03:38,892
The remaining gap is login itself — when
you need a standardized ID token that says
“this browser session is Alice,” not only
“this client may call these harbor APIs.”
That is OpenID Connect.
